        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Black widow spider (Latrodectusspp.)— four species indigenous to the United States (L. mactans,L. geometricus,L. hesperus,L. variolus); females toxic; 2–2.5 cm in length; shiny black; red or orange hourglass mark on the ventral abdomen; immature female brown with red to orange stripes that change into the hourglass shape as she darkens to black and ages.",
              "pdf_page": 1356,
              "printed_page": 1274,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bites—15% may be dry (no venom injected).",
              "pdf_page": 1356,
              "printed_page": 1274,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Range—genus found in every state except Alaska; often found around buildings and human habitation.",
              "pdf_page": 1356,
              "printed_page": 1274,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Venom—containsα-latrotoxin, a potent presynaptic neurotoxin; opens cation-selective channels at the nerve terminal; causes massive release of acetylcholine and norepinephrine, which causes sustained muscular spasms. Two classes of receptors forα-latrotoxin: the neurexins, which are brain-specific cell adhesion molecules binding in a Ca2+dependent manner, and latrophilins (and CIRLs), which are not brain specific, expressed in many tissues, and bind α-latrotoxin independent of Ca2+.",
              "pdf_page": 1356,
              "printed_page": 1274,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
